course: dictionary
lesson: 13618
title: Indicative: a sign, and a verb mood
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: Indicative has two meanings. In everyday and formal writing, it means showing or suggesting something, like a sign. In grammar, it names a verb form, the indicative mood.
explain: «Dark clouds are indicative of rain.» The clouds are a sign that rain is coming, so indicative here means showing or suggesting. «Her smile was indicative of how happy she felt.» Her smile shows her happiness.
explain: In grammar, the indicative is the ordinary verb form for facts and questions. «She runs to school.» and «Did it rain last night?» are both in the indicative. A command such as «Run to school!» is not.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping in a simpler word. Could you say «shows» or «suggests» instead of indicative? If yes, you have the first meaning. If the sentence is about a verb, you have the grammar meaning.
fact: Indicative means showing or suggesting something, as in «indicative of rain».
fact: In grammar, the indicative mood is used for facts and questions.
fact: «She runs» and «Did it rain?» are in the indicative.
fact: «Run!» is a command, so it is not in the indicative.
fact: Useful phrases: «indicative of», «the indicative mood», «present indicative».
exercise: «The grey clouds are indicative of rain.» What does indicative mean here? | Hidden under the ground | *Showing or suggesting something | Coloured bright blue | Very heavy
exercise: «Run to the shop!» Is this verb in the indicative mood? | Yes, because it has a verb in it | Yes, it asks a question | *No, it is a command, not a fact or question | Yes, it states a fact
exercise: Which sentence is in the indicative mood? | Please be quiet. | Close the door! | *She plays the piano every day | I wish it were summer
test: «Her puffy eyes are indicative of crying.» What does indicative mean? | Being sad | Very loud | *Showing or suggesting something | Hidden away
test: Which sentence is a plain fact in the indicative mood? | Open the window! | Let us go now. | Be careful on the ice. | *It rained all night.
exam: Which sentence is in the indicative mood, and what does it tell us? | Would that I were rich | Shut the gate, please | Stop talking! | *The sun rises in the east, a plain fact
